    "q-42635": {
      "dom_id": "step-q42635",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are two events such that $P(A)=0.6,$ $P(B)=0.3,$ and $P(A\\cap B)=0.2,$ find $P(A\\cup B).$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275667/assets/35e807b7de787f50d0cbff770e4083cef84b4d35030b4c506b1db5646cf7a358.png)\n\nThe addition law for probability states that\n\n$$\nP(A\\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B).\n$$\n\nSo, to calculate $P(A\\cup B),$ we apply the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\  & =0.6+0.3-0.2 \\\\  & =0.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Venn diagram corresponding to this situation is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275667/assets/dd48e67937234e9344b8b5440c69e6601c942580f7775394a43274aac26be181.png)",
